# Development Services

`docker-compose.yml` includes optional local services that stay dormant until
their exact Compose profile is enabled. Change only the owner-controlled
`COMPOSE_PROFILES` value in `.env`; the project does not need to be rendered
again.

```env
COMPOSE_PROFILES=redis,rustfs,opensearch
```

Redis has no generated password or ACL and retains the existing
`IP_ADDRESS=0.0.0.0` compatibility default. Before enabling `redis` on an
untrusted network, set `IP_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1`. External Redis access requires
an owner-managed Compose override that configures authentication together with
matching App connection settings.

Then start the selected services:

```bash
docker-compose up -d
```

| Profile | Default endpoints | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `redis` | `127.0.0.1:6379` after applying the safety setting above | Shared local Redis service without generated authentication |
| `rustfs` | S3 API `http://127.0.0.1:9000`; console `http://127.0.0.1:9001` | Credentials come from `RUSTFS_ACCESS_KEY` and `RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY` |
| `opensearch` | API `https://127.0.0.1:9200`; analyzer `127.0.0.1:9600`; Dashboards `http://127.0.0.1:5601` | Development TLS uses a demo certificate; log in as `admin` with the initial password described below |

The corresponding `*_PORT` assignments in `.env` override these host ports.
RustFS and OpenSearch ports bind to `127.0.0.1` by default. Change
`DEV_SERVICE_IP_ADDRESS` only when another host must connect. Redis retains the
project's `IP_ADDRESS` setting for compatibility; set it to `127.0.0.1` to
restrict Redis too. Replace the development credentials before exposing any
service beyond the local machine.

Quote edited secrets with single quotes in `.env` so spaces remain intact and
characters such as `$` and `#` are not interpreted as dotenv syntax:

```env
RUSTFS_SECRET_KEY='replace-with-a-local-secret'
OPENSEARCH_INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD='G0Forj-Choose-A-New-Local-2026!'
```

## Project-Specific Profiles

Additional tools belong in owner-managed `docker-compose.override.yml`. Give
each added service an exact `profiles` entry and add that token to
`COMPOSE_PROFILES`. GoForj preserves the override file and unknown profile
tokens, so project-specific services follow the same no-rerender activation
workflow as the built-in catalog.

## RustFS And S3 Storage

The `rustfs` profile starts an S3-compatible server; it does not silently
change the App's storage driver or create a bucket. Create the bucket through
the RustFS console, then configure a disk that already includes the `s3`
driver:

```env
COMPOSE_PROFILES=rustfs
STORAGE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS=local,s3
STORAGE_DRIVER=s3
STORAGE_BUCKET=app
STORAGE_REGION=us-east-1
STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://rustfs:9000
STORAGE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=rustfsadmin
STORAGE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=rustfsadmin
STORAGE_USE_PATH_STYLE=true
```

Commands running directly on the host cannot resolve the Compose hostname. Add
`STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:9000` to `.env.host` while retaining
`STORAGE_ENDPOINT=http://rustfs:9000` in `.env`. If `s3` is not already in
`STORAGE_SUPPORTED_DRIVERS`, add it and run `forj generate --storage` before
selecting it; starting RustFS does not widen the compiled App driver contract.

## OpenSearch Host Requirements

OpenSearch needs at least 4 GB available to Docker. Linux and WSL hosts also
need `vm.max_map_count=262144`; follow the OpenSearch Docker installation
guide for the persistent host setting. The generated single-node stack keeps
OpenSearch security enabled, uses a demo TLS certificate, and is intended only
for local development.

When calling the local HTTPS API, use the `admin` account and configure the
client to accept the demo certificate because it is not trusted by the host.

`OPENSEARCH_INITIAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` bootstraps a fresh OpenSearch data volume;
it is not a password-rotation setting. Choose it before the first start.
Changing only `.env` after initialization leaves the stored security index
unchanged and makes the generated health check use the wrong password. Rotate
an existing password with OpenSearch security tooling and update `.env` at the
same time. For a disposable local cluster, stop Compose and remove only its
OpenSearch named volume to reinitialize it; that permanently deletes its local
indexes.

Removing a token from `COMPOSE_PROFILES` changes the next Compose selection but
does not stop an already-created container. After disabling a profile, run
`forj down` or the all-profile teardown below before starting the new selection.

`docker-compose --profile "*" down` stops the entire stack without deleting
its named volumes. Profile changes and normal teardown never delete service
data.
